;; Section 2 top MMA kernel — Intel BMG, float16.
;;
;; SUBGROUP COUNT x K-DEPTH ARE THE TUNED PARAMETERS, AND THEY ARE NOT INDEPENDENT.  This kernel
;; distributes a 128x256 output tile across SIXTEEN subgroups and walks K in steps of 32.  Both
;; halves of that are required; either alone loses.
;;
;; Measured on an Arc B580 through benchmarks/matmul/crisp/bench_harness_l0.cpp, TFLOPS,
;; every point MMA_CORRECT.  ALL NUMBERS BELOW ARE LINUX (the Docker bench image, oneAPI +
;; L0 over /dev/dxg) -- see the platform note further down, which is not a footnote.
;;
;;     N                        1024    2048    4096    8192
;;     this kernel (16sg K=32)  63.5    56.4    64.5    66.2
;;     the kernel it replaces   49.0    60.0    57.8    40.3
;;
;; +29.5% / -6.0% / +11.7% / +64.1%, geometric mean +22.2%.  Measured back to back in ONE
;; container run, so the comparison is not carrying any cross-session drift.  Note how
;; closely these track the bf16 twin's deltas -- same geometry, same engine.
;;
;; THE 2048 CELL IS A REAL LOSS AND IS BEING ACCEPTED, not explained away.  It is outside
;; the 2.2% spread at that size.  It buys +64% at 8192, which is where the comparison
;; against the peers is actually decided, and +30% at 1024.
;;
;; RUN-TO-RUN SPREAD, six repeats of this kernel, so deltas can be read against something:
;;     1024 2.2%   2048 2.2%   4096 3.1%   8192 0.7%
;; A 6% difference at 2048 is therefore signal, not noise.  One screen reading of 61.7 at
;; 2048 sits outside the six-repeat band (56.6-57.8) and is unexplained; it is the only
;; measurement here that does not reconcile.
;;
;; THE PLATFORM MATTERS MORE THAN ANY KNOB IN THIS FILE.  The same unchanged kernel measures
;; very differently on Windows-native L0 and on Linux in the bench container:
;;
;;     N            1024    2048    4096    8192
;;     Windows      69.3    62.3    56.0    51.3
;;     Linux        63.1    57.7    64.5    66.2
;;
;; Windows wins the small sizes, Linux wins the large ones, and at 8192 the gap is 29%.  A
;; candidate ranking taken on one platform is NOT evidence about the other -- w64_k64 (one
;; subgroup, 32x64 tile, K=64) led the Windows screen at 59.1 @4096 and collapses to 26.9 on
;; Linux, last of six.  The Windows screen that originally chose this geometry has been
;; re-run here; the choice survives, but it was luck that it did.  Screen on Linux.
;;
;; K DEPTH IS NOT MONOTONE.  Doubling K from the optimum regresses in both geometries.
;; There is a K optimum per geometry and it moves with subgroup count.  Do not tune one
;; without re-tuning the other.
;;
;; STANDING vs THE CEILING.  At 8192 this reads 66.1 against oneMKL's 111.7 -- 59% of it, up
;; from 33%.  There is no SYCL-TLA fp16 contender wired up, so the peer column is empty here;
;; the bf16 chapter carries that comparison.
;;
;; CORRECTED, AND THE REASON IT IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN DELETED.  The kernel this replaces recorded
;; as a measured negative: "multi-subgroup 256x256 over 32 subgroups = 34.6 TFLOPS @2048 vs this
;; kernel's 60.8.  The geometry SYCL-TLA uses; it is not what makes SYCL-TLA fast."  That was an
;; honest reading of an honest measurement -- taken at K=16.  The geometry was never the problem;
;; there was not enough work in flight per subgroup to pay for the participants.  A note saying
;; "tried it, it lost" is what stops the next person re-running it, so it has to be the note that
;; gets fixed when the finding turns over.
;;
;; NO RING, NO PREFETCH, NO BARRIER.  All three were in the kernel this replaces and none survives:
;;   ring          the ring costs registers that K depth spends better.  Two native K-steps inside
;;                 one tile are already two independent MMA chains; a second buffer buys nothing and
;;                 at K=32 across 16 subgroups it does not fit.  (Crisp's :ring-count is a BUFFER
;;                 count; SYCL-TLA's Stages is a PREFETCH DEPTH over one buffer.  Not the same knob.)
;;   prefetch      warms a line for one consumer.  With 16 subgroups sharing a workgroup tile the
;;                 hardware is already getting the reuse the prefetch was buying.
;;   barrier       endeavour 157 measured this directly: a fused (sync-workgroup) in the K loop costs
;;                 14.7% at 16 subgroups, and the split form -- which removes ~90% of that cost --
;;                 still does not beat no barrier at all.  See tests/spec/157-split-barrier/.
;;
;; WHAT DID NOT WORK, so nobody re-tries them blind.  Measured at the single-subgroup 32x64 geometry
;; unless noted; retained because they cost real hardware time:
;;   ring depth 3     +9% at 32x32 but COLLAPSES 32x64 (57 -> 23 TFLOPS)
;;   prefetch dist 3  1.38x vs base, i.e. worse than 32x64's 1.70x
;;   prefetch dist 4  0.80x — actively harmful
;;   TM 64 (64x32)    does not run at all
;;   TN 96            MMA_WRONG at 0.27 TFLOPS — the GPU-fault signature, under investigation
;;   TN 128           correct but 0.14x
;;   SLM staging      correct but 40x SLOWER (0.9 vs 41.9 @2048).  SPV_INTEL_2d_block_io is a
;;                    GLOBAL-memory facility, so staging operands through shared local memory
;;                    loses the 2D block load entirely (load_block2d 41 -> 0).
;;   :xe-native+ring  the second lowering does NOT compose with the ring: 50.0 @2048 with ring 2,
;;                    against 57.7 for :xe-native bare.
;;   address hoisting cut emulated 64-bit multiplies 36% for +2.9%, and on the ring kernel gave
;;                    identical instruction counts 17% SLOWER.  Not issue-bound.
;;
;; THE LOWERING.  This kernel uses the default :coop-matrix path.  Endeavour 156 added a second,
;; (mma-lowering :xe-native) -- Intel's SubgroupMatrixMultiplyAccumulateINTEL with 2D block loads and
;; a VNNI-transform load for B.  It is a better lowering at matched geometry (+13% @2048, +9% @4096)
;; and produces leaner code (42 instructions per dpas vs 57), but it has not been re-tried against
;; THIS geometry.  That is now the most promising untested combination we have.  Retry it when the
;; :xe-native load path stops degrading with live fragment count (32x128 collapses to 5.6 TFLOPS; it
;; is NOT register spill -- the ISA shows zero).
;;
;; ON LARGE-GRF.  Endeavour 144 selects the register mode per kernel and the harness must ask the JIT
;; for the same one.  Note the selection is geometry-dependent and was once read as a general law:
;; large-GRF is worth ~2x to the single-subgroup 32x32 shape, while at 128x128 over 16 subgroups the
;; DEFAULT allocation beats it by 70% (32.2 -> 54.9 @4096).  SYCL-TLA is INDIFFERENT to the choice --
;; forced from grf_count 128 (with 3904 bytes of deliberate spill) to 256 with none, it went 185 ->
;; 190 TFLOPS.  Avoiding spill is worth ~3x to OUR lowering and nothing to its.  That is a property
;; of our codegen, not of the hardware.
;;
;; STANDING vs THE PEER.  At 8192 this reads 53.2 against SYCL-TLA's 235.8 -- 23% of it, up from the
;; 16% the previous kernel managed.  The remaining gap is a LOWERING gap, not a geometry gap: the
;; peer uses inline Xe asm DPAS with createBlock2DAddressPayload and never materialises a 64-bit
;; address.  Geometry is now approximately matched; the instruction stream is not.
;;
;; FP16 vs BF16.  Identical geometry; only the operand element type differs.  Measured side by side
;; on this hardware, fp16 reads 69.1 / 60.9 / 58.1 / 53.2 against bf16's 69.3 / 62.3 / 56.0 / 51.3 --
;; within 4% either way, crossing over around 4096.  That is expected: XMX takes the same (8 16 16)
;; shape for both and accumulates in f32 either way, so the choice is about the numerics of the
;; operands, not the throughput of the engine.  Do not read the crossover as signal; it is inside
;; the spread these two have shown across re-runs.
(declaim (precision fast))
(def-type a-mat (matrix half :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type b-mat (matrix half :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type c-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel matmul (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(a-mat b-mat &out c-mat)
           (global-size :derive-from C :strategy :strided)
           (local-size :set-to 256))
  (let ((A-tile (make-register-tile half (128 32) 0.0 :operand :a :warps '(true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true)))
        (B-tile (make-register-tile half (32 256) 0.0 :operand :b :warps '(true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true)))
        (C-tile (make-register-tile float (128 256) 0.0 :warps '(true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true true)))
        (K (inner-dimension A B)))
    (tile-stride C (128 256) (grid-y grid-x)
      (progn
        (dotimes (grid-k (/ (to-ulong K) (to-ulong 32)))
          (load-tile A A-tile (grid-y grid-k))
          (load-tile B B-tile (grid-k grid-x))
          (mma-accumulate-via-tile (8 16 16) C-tile A-tile B-tile))
        :epilogue
        (store-tile C-tile C (grid-y grid-x))))))
